= Testing Mungo Caverns =

Five suites and a harness:

  ../test.shoddy        the generator, the table sizes, the parser, and
                        the output channel.  The fast one.
  test-tables.shoddy    what must be true of the cave itself: every
                        travel row leads somewhere real, every block of
                        rules ends in a stop, every room you can stand in
                        describes itself, every hint costs points, and
                        which rooms the travel table does not connect.
  test-turn.shoddy      whole turns, driven headless.  A turn is
                        Command(Game, String) -> Done, so a scenario is a
                        function call and not a recorded session: take
                        the lamp, stand in the dark, unlock the grate,
                        suspend and resume, and ask the game what it did.
  test-walk.shoddy      one game played from the road to the gold and
                        back, with the score checked at every landmark.
                        Thirteen commands through the parser, the travel
                        table, two forced rooms, the darkness rule, the
                        lamp and the scorer: one wrong number anywhere
                        and the walk lands somewhere else.
  test-fuzz.shoddy      450 commands drawn at random from the game's own
                        vocabulary, checking only that the game is still
                        a game after each one.  Deterministic: the
                        commands come from the same generator, run from
                        a separate seed threaded alongside the game.
  harness.shoddy        the game, plus the twelve lines of turn loop the
                        suites above need.  They include this and
                        nothing else.

    ./build.sh test     runs all five               (~20 seconds)

194 assertions in 29 check groups, and the count understates them: most
of test-tables.shoddy asserts through All(Range(...)) sweeps, so its four
travel-row assertions are four predicates across all 878 rows, and the
rest sweep the 185 locations, the 70 objects, the 213 messages and the
vocabulary the same way.  What runs is on the order of ten thousand
predicate evaluations.

Two notes on running them.  The suites are run with their input closed,
because dying asks whether you want to be resurrected and that is a
read; with no input the answer is no, which is why a fuzz run reports
how far it got -- on the run we ship, one leg is killed by a curmudgeon's
knife after 43 of its 150 commands, and says so rather than hiding it.
And test-turn.shoddy writes and re-reads one save file,
tests/roundtrip.adv, which is how the save format's round trip is
checked; it is the only thing here that touches the disk, and it is
gitignored.


= On the recorded transcripts =

Open Adventure, the C this was ported from, ships about 107 recorded
transcripts: a .log of commands paired with the .chk of exact output it
produces.  Ninety-two of them open with an in-band "seed" command that
pins the linear congruential generator, which makes them a far stronger
oracle than golden files snapshotted from the current build -- a change
that draws one extra random number, or skips one draw, diverges every
seeded game from that point on, and a golden file taken from today's
mungo-caverns would happily bless the divergence.

The port was developed against them and reached byte-for-byte agreement
on all 107.  That is why the tables are as faithful as they are, and it
is why the draw order is treated as load-bearing throughout the source.

They are not shipped here and there is nothing to run.  They belong to
Open Adventure, and everything they graded has since been embodied in
the five suites above, which are the specification now.  Replaying them
also needed three adjustments a reader should know were made rather than
assumed away: the curmudgeons' upstream name was substituted in both
directions, the game's own rename was mapped forward onto the expected
text, and both sides were reflowed paragraph-by-paragraph, because
"curmudgeon" is a longer word than the one it replaced and stored
message text therefore wraps at different columns.

One consequence worth keeping in mind.  The suites are a baseline: they
tell you the cave is internally coherent and that the port still does
what it did yesterday.  They cannot tell you it still matches Crowther
and Woods.  Nothing in the tree can, now, so a change to the random
stream or to stored message text is the kind that has to be reasoned
about rather than run.
